# A commit form that drafts its own words, and a sidebar naming the repository (2026-10-04)

`TODO.md` said the AI commit-message suggestion and its edit form were "尚未实现". They are
implemented now: the host reads the staged diff through the Git adapter, hands it to
whatever Provider the host was started with, cleans the answer, and offers it in an editable
commit box that still requires the two confirmations before anything is committed. Driving
that in a real browser also turned up a second, older defect that has nothing to do with
commit messages: on a host started with `CHAOS_WORKSPACE_ROOT`, the sidebar never listed the
workspace the session was sitting in, so nothing on screen said which repository the panels
were pointed at. Both are measured here.

Headline figures: 14 engine tests in the new group, 3 WebSocket tests against a self-built
inference endpoint plus the 4 in `host_info_flow.rs`, 59 reducer tests
(`apps/chaos-ui/src/session.test.ts`, 13 of them added across this feature), 123 vitest tests
overall, 2 browser spec files holding 7 cases on two viewports (14 browser cases), the full
browser suite at 75 passed / 6 skipped plus 14 passed, and 28 source mutants of which 27 are
caught by a named test and 1 only fails to compile -- which is why the replay test has a
second mutant aimed at the same behaviour. Section 10 and 11 are the Safe Web Mode round;
section 12 is what is still not covered.

== 0. Nothing outside a test module could see the staged diff

`GitAdapter` had `stage`, `unstage`, `commit`, `checkout_branch` and `discard`. There was no
way to read what a commit *would* record, so any suggestion would have had to be written
from the file names alone. The trait gained one defaulted method, so the fixtures that only
assert on mutations keep compiling and answer "this adapter cannot read the staged diff"
instead of lying:

    fn staged_diff(&self) -> Result<StagedDiff, String> {
        Err("Git adapter 不支持读取暂存差异".into())
    }

`ProcessGitAdapter` implements it by spawning `git -C <cwd> diff --cached --no-color` and
draining both pipes on their own threads -- git that could not write to stderr would block,
and the `wait()` behind it would never return. The read is bounded to `COMMIT_DIFF_LIMIT`
(24 KiB) because the diff goes into a prompt and the browser can stage a generated file of
any size; when it stops short the result says so, and that flag travels all the way to the
browser panel.

Two things the tests pin that are easy to get wrong and hard to notice:

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --lib -- commit_message staged_diff normalize_commit \
        empty_stage a_provider_failure a_replayed a_suggestion_built

    running 10 tests
    ..........
    test result: ok. 10 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 192 filtered out; finished in 0.02s

`staged_diff_reports_only_what_is_staged` edits the same file twice, stages one of the two
edits, and requires `+two` in the text and `+three` absent. Reading `git diff` instead of
`git diff --cached` passes that test's first half and fails its second, which is mutant M1
below. `staged_diff_stops_at_the_limit_and_says_so` stages a 48 KiB change and asserts the
flag, the `[diff truncated]` marker, and that the retained text is not over the limit --
`truncated` is a claim about bytes, so it is checked against bytes rather than about the
model's opinion.

== 1. What a model wraps around an answer

The answer arrives as SSE deltas. A chatty model wraps it in a code fence, bullets it, or
opens with 「这是建议：」, and all of that would otherwise be pasted into `git commit -m`.
`normalize_commit_message` strips fence lines, leading blanks, one leading label line of at
most 40 bytes ending in `:` or `：`, bullet and colon prefixes, and surrounding quotes; it
keeps everything after that, body paragraphs included. Dropping a paragraph the Provider
judged worth writing is a worse failure than leaving one explanatory line the user can
delete in the box. The result is cut at `COMMIT_MESSAGE_LIMIT` (600 bytes) on a character
boundary.

Both ends wrap and unwrap for real rather than agreeing on a clean fixture. The browser's
mock endpoint answers in three fenced frames, and the WebSocket test's scripted reply is
fenced too:

    // apps/chaos-ui/e2e/support/mock-provider.mjs
    const replyFrames = ['```text\n', replyText, '\n```']

    // crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/tests/commit_message_flow.rs
    provider.set_response("```text\ndocs: 改写 note.txt 的说明\n\n旧措辞描述的是上一个版本。\n```");

Both then assert the *unfenced* string. Skipping the normalisation call is mutant M9; it
survives the engine group (its fake answers in plain text) and is caught by the WebSocket
test, which is the reason that reply is fenced.

== 2. Two ways the feature can be missing, named separately

An empty staged area and an unconfigured Provider both end in an error frame, but they are
not the same problem for the person at the keyboard, and neither is answered by asking the
endpoint:

    ClientMessage::SuggestCommitMessage { ... } => {
        ...
        if diff.text.trim().is_empty() {
            return vec![Self::error("nothing_staged", "暂存区为空，先 stage 文件再请求提交信息建议")];
        }
        let Some(adapter) = &self.adapter else {
            return vec![Self::error(
                "commit_suggestion_unavailable",
                "没有配置 Provider，无法生成提交信息建议；提交信息仍可手动填写",
            )];
        };

The order matters and is asserted: `empty_stage_is_named_before_the_provider_is_asked` uses
an engine with no Provider at all and still expects `nothing_staged`, so a missing Provider
cannot hide the fact that there was nothing to suggest about. The browser spec checks the
same ordering on the wire: after 建议提交信息 on an unstaged file, the panel alerts
暂存区为空 *and* the endpoint's prompt log is still empty, so the refusal is proven to have
happened before any request left. A Provider that answers 401 surfaces as `agent_failed`
with the endpoint's text, and one that answers with whitespace-only text is
`commit_suggestion_empty`; neither is delivered as an empty suggestion, which is what the
browser would otherwise show as a blank commit message.

== 3. A suggestion is wording, not an action

The handler emits `Ack` + `CommitMessageSuggestion` and bumps `session.sequence` only. It
requests no approval, runs no command, and the tests check the repository rather than the
intent: HEAD must still point at the commit made before the request, `git diff --cached
--name-only` must still list the staged file, and no `ToolApprovalRequested` may appear in
the events. The browser spec goes one step further and commits after the suggestion,
asserting that `git log -1 --format=%s` records the message the *user* left in the box
(`chore: 我自己写的提交信息`), not the Provider's wording.

The box is only filled while it still holds what it held when the request went out
(`commitDraftAcceptsSuggestion`), so a suggestion that lands late is offered beside the box
instead of overwriting typed text. That branch is reached without sleeping: the mock
endpoint holds its reply while a hold file exists, the spec types 我自己的草稿, removes the
file, and then asserts `commit-suggestion-offer` shows the wording while the box keeps the
draft, and that 填入建议 is what finally puts it in.

== 4. What the browser found that was not about commit messages

`openCommitForm` had been opening the Git tab without checking the sidebar, and a screenshot
taken to inspect the new form showed the sidebar section 工作区与会话 empty. Reproduced as
an assertion rather than left as a pixel:

    $ npx playwright test --config playwright.git.config.ts --grep "fits the screen" --repeat-each=3

    Error: expect(locator).toBeVisible() failed
    Locator: getByRole('button', { name: '默认工作区' })
    Expected: visible
    Timeout: 5000ms
    Error: element(s) not found

6 failures out of 6, on both viewports. The cause is an ordering in `connect()`: the client
asks for the workspace list as the socket opens and then, in the same tick, sends
`create_session` -- and on a fresh host that is the message that creates the fallback
workspace. The list was answered before the thing it would list existed, and `CreateSession`
returns only `SessionCreated`, so the sidebar was never told. (The breadcrumb looked correct
the whole time, which is why nobody had noticed: it reads the workspace name off the
session, not off the list.)

The client fix is a state-driven re-ask rather than a reordering, so it also covers a
workspace appearing by any other route, and it asks at most once per active workspace id so
a host that keeps answering without it cannot turn this into a request loop:

    const workspaceId = session.activeWorkspaceId
    if (!session.sessionId || !workspaceId) return
    if (session.workspaces.some((workspace) => workspace.id === workspaceId)) return
    if (workspaceListAskedFor.current === workspaceId) return
    workspaceListAskedFor.current = workspaceId
    send({ type: 'list_workspaces', client_msg_id: crypto.randomUUID() })

An engine-side push (appending `Workspaces` to the `CreateSession` answer, which is what
`CreateWorkspace` already does) was tried and rejected on measured cost: five WebSocket
tests read frames positionally after `create_session` (`workspace_diff_flow.rs` alone has
nine `next_message` reads), so the extra frame would have needed nine `let _ =
next_message(...)` lines that each weaken an assertion. The client asks, and the protocol
stays as it was.

With the fix in, all 8 browser cases pass, and the assertion is not decorative: deleting the
`send` line from the new effect brings the failure back at both viewports.

    M  workspace row effect: send({ type: 'list_workspaces', ... }) removed
       -> ✘ [desktop-chromium] an empty staged area is refused before the endpoint is asked
       -> ✘ [mobile-chromium]  an empty staged area is refused before the endpoint is asked

The full suite was re-run afterwards to make sure a new in-flight `list_workspaces` request
breaks nothing that reads the sidebar: 75 passed / 6 skipped on the main config plus 8 on
this one, with the phone-shell drawer and workspace-isolation specs included.

== 5. Screenshots, actually looked at

Layout was inspected at 1440x1000 and 390x844 (six captures, reviewed). The commit form at
phone width keeps the textarea, both buttons and the note inside the panel with no
horizontal overflow; the late-arrival state at 390 shows the draft still in the box with
「Provider 建议：...」 under it, and the refusal state shows the placeholder
「自己写，或点「建议提交信息」让 Provider 起草」 plus the alert text. The screenshot setting
was flipped for the capture and restored byte-identically (`cmp` clean), so nothing in the
committed config captures images on a green run. The layout facts are also pinned as a
durable case rather than left to the eye: 建议提交信息, 填入建议, the textarea and 执行 Git
操作 are each asserted to have a bounding box inside the viewport, and
`document.documentElement.scrollWidth - window.innerWidth` must be <= 0.

== 6. Self-built endpoint and throwaway repository

The suite needs a host started with a Provider, and `CHAOS_PROVIDER_*` replaces the demo
responder every other spec asserts on, so it runs under a second Playwright config that
`e2e-runner.mjs` runs right after the first one; `npm run test:e2e` is still the single
command, and CI needs no new job. The endpoint (`e2e/support/mock-provider.mjs`) speaks the
wire format the shipped `HttpPromptAdapter` parses: bearer auth, `/v1/models`, SSE `data:
{"choices":[{"delta":...}]}` frames, and each request body appended to a prompt log the
specs read back. The repository is rebuilt per test under the ignored
`.chaos/e2e-git-workspace/` -- one commit, one staged edit, one deliberately unstaged edit
-- so nothing a commit test writes can land in the tracked tree.

    $ cd apps/chaos-ui && npx playwright test --config playwright.git.config.ts

      ✓  1 [desktop-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:62:1 › a suggested message fills the commit form and only an approved commit writes (979ms)
      ✓  2 [desktop-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:93:1 › an empty staged area is refused before the endpoint is asked (721ms)
      ✓  3 [desktop-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:104:1 › the commit form fits the screen it is shown on (838ms)
      ✓  4 [desktop-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:129:1 › a suggestion that lands after the user typed is offered beside the box (856ms)
      ✓  5 [mobile-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:62:1 › a suggested message fills the commit form and only an approved commit writes (1.1s)
      ✓  6 [mobile-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:93:1 › an empty staged area is refused before the endpoint is asked (834ms)
      ✓  7 [mobile-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:104:1 › the commit form fits the screen it is shown on (835ms)
      ✓  8 [mobile-chromium] › e2e/commit-message.pw.ts:129:1 › a suggestion that lands after the user typed is offered beside the box (934ms)

      8 passed (8.7s)

The prompt the browser caused to be sent is asserted on the endpoint side, not the client
side: exactly one `/chat/completions` call per click, `Authorization: Bearer
sk-commit-token-0123456789`, the body containing `+第二版说明` between the two `STAGED DIFF`
delimiters and the branch line, and `third, unstaged` absent from it.

== 7. Mutations

Each mutant is applied to a source, the targeted suite runs, and the source is restored and
compared with `cmp`; the driver aborts on a restore mismatch or a non-green baseline.
Failing test names are the ones the driver recorded, not the ones expected afterwards.

    M1  staged read becomes `git diff`              → staged_diff_reports_only_what_is_staged,
                                                       staged_diff_stops_at_the_limit_and_says_so,
                                                       and 6 more in the group
    M2  read limit multiplied by 8                  → staged_diff_stops_at_the_limit_and_says_so,
                                                       a_suggestion_built_from_a_truncated_diff_says_so
    M3  branch line renamed in the prompt           → commit_message_prompt_marks_a_truncated_diff
    M4  empty-stage guard removed                   → empty_stage_is_named_before_the_provider_is_asked
                                                       (and both viewports in the browser)
    M5  fence stripping disabled                    → normalize_commit_message_removes_..._an_answer
    M6  error code renamed to agent_failed          → commit_message_suggestion_names_the_missing_half
