21 mutants, 20 caught by a named test, 1 caught only by the compiler. M7 is recorded rather
than counted: dropping a variant from that match arm makes the match non-exhaustive, so the
run dies before any test executes and proves nothing about the replay behaviour. M10 asks
the same question in a way that compiles -- exempt exactly this message from the dedup gate
-- and `a_replayed_suggestion_request_asks_the_provider_once` goes red on it, which is what
makes that test's bite real. N1 and S1 are the two properties that came out of this batch
after the feature itself was done: §4 records S1, and §8 records N1 together with the fact
that the first version of its test survived this same mutant by accident. M11 to M13 and T4
to T6 are the second round, added after the full gate run found the two gaps §9 records; each
was applied to the source the same way as the rows above, with a green baseline before and a
green restored tree after. N0 is absent from
this table because it is not a mutant of the shipped source -- it is the un-enrolled form this
code had before the workspace ban on `Command::spawn` rejected it, which is a verdict from the
lint rather than from a test (§8).

== 8. A git read that can spawn, and a test that passed for the wrong reason

`git diff --cached` is not a leaf process when the repository says otherwise: a
`diff <name>.command` entry in the repo's own config, picked up by a `.gitattributes` line,
makes git exec a program the repository chose, and that program can leave something running
after it exits. The first version of `staged_diff()` spawned git directly. The workspace bans
that call, so this surfaced on the build leg before any test could run. The diagnostic,
reproduced from the shipped source by deleting the enrolled block:

    $ cargo clippy -q -p chaos-engine --lib -- -D warnings

    error: use of a disallowed method `std::process::Command::spawn`
       --> crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs:276:14
        |
    276 |             .spawn()
        |              ^^^^^
        |
        = note: an unenrolled child outlives its session; use xai_tty_utils::ProcessScope::enroll
    (… 3 lines elided here: the lint-name help, the implied-by note, the override help)

    error: could not compile `chaos-engine` (lib) due to 1 previous error

The shape it was fixed into is the one `ProcessTerminalAdapter::run` in the same file already
uses -- start the child in a process group of its own, enroll it in a scope, and kill that
group once `wait()` has returned:

    xai_tty_utils::detach_std_command(&mut builder);
    let process_scope = xai_tty_utils::ProcessScope::new();
    #[allow(clippy::disallowed_methods)] // reaped by wait() below and kill_all() after it
    let mut child = builder.spawn().map_err(|error| format!("无法启动 git: {error}"))?;
    let _process_group = match process_scope.enroll_std(&child) {
        Ok(group) => group,
        Err(error) => { let _ = child.kill(); let _ = child.wait(); return Err(…); }
    };
    (… 15 lines elided here: the comment about draining, and taking and draining both pipes)
    let status = child.wait().map_err(|error| format!("等待 git 失败: {error}"))?;
    process_scope.kill_all();

`ProcessScope` has no `Drop` impl, so that last line is a call someone has to write, not
something the borrow checker guarantees, and the `allow` carries the reason the ban asks for.

What was missing was a test for it.
`a_diff_driver_background_process_does_not_outlive_the_staged_diff_read` writes a driver
script that backgrounds `sleep 120` and records its pid, points the repository's own diff
config at it (`diff.chaos.command`, selected by `*.txt diff=chaos`), calls the real
`staged_diff()`, then asks `/proc/<pid>/stat` whether that pid is still alive -- a missing
`/proc` entry is death and a `Z` state is not life. It is `#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]` for
exactly those two reasons, and the reason is written into its row in
`scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv`, which is what keeps the ledger gate green:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-platform-gated-tests.py
    (… 2 lines elided here: the per-test progress dots and the timing rule line)
    Ran 42 tests in 15.038s

    OK

    $ python3 scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.py --quiet --check-baseline \
        scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv --max-unreviewed 1106 --max-blind-windows 74 \
        --max-blind-macos 11 --max-assumption-free 427

    (no output, exit code 0)

The first version of that test was vacuous, and the only thing that found it was a mutant
surviving. With `process_scope.kill_all();` deleted it went green, after 120 seconds:

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --lib -- a_diff_driver

    running 1 test
    test tests::a_diff_driver_background_process_does_not_outlive_the_staged_diff_read has been running for over 60 seconds
    .
    test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 202 filtered out; finished in 120.04s

Nothing was reaped in that run; the sleep simply ran out its own 120 seconds. The reason the
read could take that long was the driver script rather than the code under test: `sleep 120 &`
inherits git's stdout, which is the write end of the very pipe this read drains, so the reader
thread saw no EOF until the sleep exited on its own. The lifetime of the read was bounded by
the leaked process instead of by the reaping, and by the time the test looked, the pid was
gone for a reason unrelated to `kill_all()`. Redirecting the background process's own
descriptors (`sleep 120 >/dev/null 2>&1 &`) takes the pipe out of the picture:

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --lib -- staged_diff a_diff_driver

    running 4 tests
    ....
    test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 199 filtered out; finished in 0.02s

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --lib -- a_diff_driver

    thread 'tests::a_diff_driver_background_process_does_not_outlive_the_staged_diff_read' (372006) panicked at crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs:4905:9:
    pid 372022 outlived the staged-diff read; nothing reaped the driver's process group
    (… 6 lines elided here: the backtrace note, two blank lines and the failures list)
    test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 202 filtered out; finished in 5.06s

0.02 s green against 5.06 s red is the pair that makes the assertion worth keeping; 120 s on
both sides was not a test. Each mutated source was restored and compared with `cmp`
(byte-identical after all three runs), and the process the vacuous run leaked was a `sleep`
that ended on its own -- a scan of `/proc` afterwards found no process from any of these runs
still alive.

== 9. Two gaps the full gate found that a filtered run did not

Before shipping, the batch went through `bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build`. The
engine and web legs of this feature had been run earlier with a name filter -- `cargo test -p
chaos-engine --lib -- commit` and the commit-suggestion socket test by name -- and both had
been green, which left the impression that the touched crates were covered. They were not.
Adding a protocol message breaks two guards that carry no word of the feature in their names,
and the whole-target run is the only thing that said so:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
    (elided above: the doctest summaries of the crates that passed, printed before the verdict)

    error: 2 targets failed:
        `-p chaos-engine --lib`
        `-p xai-grok-web --test host_info_flow`
    PASS  GUI protocol types

    FAILED gates: cargo test
    39 gate(s) run, 0 skipped, 1 failed

The first failure was in the guard whose whole job is to be told about a new message. The
refusal list had gained `suggest_commit_message`, but the sample list the guard asks about had
not:

    $ cargo test -p chaos-engine --lib

    thread 'tests::safe_mode_policy_never_refuses_the_read_only_surface' (672379) panicked at crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/lib.rs:5526:21:
    suggest_commit_message is listed as refused but no client message carries that tag

    test result: FAILED. 202 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 5.12s

`every_client_message()` needed the new variant and `assert_eq!(tags.len(), 33, ...)` needed
to become 34. Without that sample the guard is not merely red -- it is the specific kind of
green that means nothing: a refusal named in the list, with no message in the test corpus
carrying that tag, would leave `safe_mode_allows` untested for the one message this batch
added.

The second failure was the same shape one crate over. `host_info_flow.rs` holds a sample per
protocol tag and drives each one through a real safe-mode socket:

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-web --test host_info_flow

    thread 'every_protocol_message_has_a_sample_to_ask_with' (668601) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/tests/host_info_flow.rs:337:5:
    these protocol messages have no sample here, so the safe-mode list was never asked about them: ["suggest_commit_message"]

    test the_refusal_list_matches_what_the_socket_actually_refuses ... FAILED
    thread 'the_refusal_list_matches_what_the_socket_actually_refuses' (668603) panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/tests/host_info_flow.rs:366:51:
    covered by the other test

    test result: FAILED. 2 passed; 2 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.02s

The second panic is the interesting one: it is not an assertion about safe mode at all, it is
`.expect("covered by the other test")` firing because the first test's premise had stopped
holding. Both went green once the sample was added, and adding it is what makes the
socket-level claim about this message real rather than intended -- that test asserts, per
protocol message, that the code coming back over the socket is exactly
`safe_web_mode_blocked`.

Reading those two failures led to a third gap, in the browser this time. `session.ts` ends the
waiting state on six error codes; the codes this handler can actually answer with are eight. A
safe-mode host -- which is the case the socket test above refuses message by message --
answers `safe_web_mode_blocked`, and a host that was restarted answers `session_not_found`.
Neither code was in the list, so the commit form would have sat on 建议生成中 forever: the
refusal is the only answer that button ever gets, and there is no suggestion coming after it.
The list is now a named constant, `commitSuggestionFailureCodes`, with all eight codes and a
note about why each belongs. On the engine side three refusals had no test at all -- an
unknown session id, an engine with no Git adapter, and a staged diff that cannot be read (the
repository removed from under a running host). Each now has one, so the engine group is 14
tests, and each is mutation-proven below.

The six mutants were driven as §7 describes -- applied to the source, the named test run, the
source restored and compared with `cmp`, driver aborting on a non-green baseline:

    summary: [('E1', 'KILLED'), ('E2', 'KILLED'), ('E3', 'KILLED'), ('U1', 'KILLED'),
    ('U2', 'KILLED'), ('U3', 'KILLED')]
    survivors: none

E1 drops the unknown-session refusal to an empty answer; U3 puts an unrelated code into the
list, so the claim being tested is the negative half -- a refusal the commit form did not ask
about must leave the request pending and settle the turn instead. Baseline was green before
the first mutation and green after the last restore; every restore compared byte-identical.
The restored runs:

    $ cargo test -p chaos-engine --lib
    test result: ok. 206 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 5.12s

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-web --test host_info_flow --test commit_message_flow
    test result: ok. 3 passed; 0 failed; ... (commit_message_flow)
    test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; ... (host_info_flow)

    $ npx vitest run src/session.test.ts
    Tests  52 passed (52)

What this section is really recording is a rule about runs, not about this feature: the two
guards that caught the gaps are whole-target tests with names that share no substring with the
feature under change, so `--lib -- commit` was a green light that meant nothing. A filtered
run proves the tests it filtered in.

== 10. A refusal that names no request, and the panels left waiting for one

Safe Web Mode is a host started with `CHAOS_SAFE_WEB_MODE` set to anything other than `0` or
`false`. It refuses twenty client messages -- every workspace and host mutation, all five
attachment messages, `list_workspaces`, `get_git_status`, `propose_terminal`,
`propose_git_mutation`, `validate_provider`, `suggest_commit_message` -- from the socket loop
in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-web/src/lib.rs`, before the engine is asked anything:

    if safe_web_mode && !safe_mode_allows(&message) {
        let event = ServerMessage::Error {
            code: "safe_web_mode_blocked".into(),
            message: "Safe Web Mode 禁止此操作".into(),
        };

That answer carries no `session_id` and nothing naming the message it refused. `Error` has
exactly those two fields in the protocol mirror, so no client can route it back to a request;
a reducer can only ask which panel is still waiting. The previous round had taught the commit
form to read the code, and no other panel had been given the same treatment.

The strand that lost was found by writing the assertions first and then taking the fix away,
on the shipped path: `apps/chaos-ui/e2e/commit-message-safe-mode.pw.ts` starts its own host on
an ephemeral port (the mode is a process-level variable, so it cannot be shared with the host
the other commit spec drives), and the driver ran it green on both viewports, replaced the
three pieces of the fix with their pre-fix forms, rebuilt `dist`, and ran it again:

    $ npx playwright test --config playwright.git.config.ts commit-message-safe-mode \
        --project=desktop-chromium --project=mobile-chromium

