Nothing had been clicked when those six went red, which is what made it a defect rather than a
gap in coverage. The page sends `list_workspaces` when the socket opens and sends it a second
time from an effect once the session exists, so the first two messages of every browser
session on this host are refused. The reducer's generic error branch turned that into 请求错误 on
the badge and settled the turn through `recordTurnOutcome`, so a conversation nobody had
prompted was recorded as a failed one. The branch now ends with a case that claims the refusal
only when no panel is waiting:

    if (refusedBySafeMode) return { ...state, status: '安全模式已拒绝' }

and the four waiting panels -- upload, suggestion, Git, terminal -- each claim their own frame
ahead of it. `gitLoading` and `terminalLoading` join `upload` and `commitSuggesting` as things
`safe_web_mode_blocked` can satisfy, because the host refuses message by message: two requests
in flight means two refusals, and the frame order decides who hears which.

Two of the three tests then read the disk, because a refusal the panel reports is still only a
client-side claim. The refused terminal command is `touch refused-by-safe-mode.txt` and the
assertion is that the file does not exist; the refused upload is a `refused.txt` whose bytes
the workspace must not contain; both tests end on the endpoint's prompt log being empty and on
`git log -1 --format=%s` still reading `base`. The third test is the control that stops those
read-backs from being a story about a dead host: a prompt is allowed on the same socket, and
the same self-built endpoint answers it -- the endpoint the suggestion was refused from is the
one that replies.

The spec also pins the tab-open behaviour, which is where the second strand had been hiding:
`goToTab('git')` calls `refreshGitStatus()` in `apps/chaos-ui/src/main.tsx`, so the first
thing the commit form has to survive is a status read nobody asked for:

    await expect(gitAlert(page, 'Git 请求失败')).toContainText(refusal)
    await expect(gitPanel(page).getByRole('status')).toHaveCount(0)

Writing those two lines surfaced a Playwright strict-mode violation: the panel can hold two
`role=alert` elements at once, the refused status read and a refused suggestion. The alert
locator in `apps/chaos-ui/e2e/support/commit-form.ts` therefore takes an optional text filter,
and that module now holds the repository fixture and every locator both commit specs share --
a copied fixture drifts and stays green.

