The commit form therefore stands at 2 spec files, 7 cases, 14 browser cases across both
viewports. `npx vitest run` is 123 tests in 9 files (59 in
`apps/chaos-ui/src/session.test.ts`, 11 in `apps/chaos-ui/src/attachments.test.ts`) and `npm
run typecheck` is clean.

One red run in this batch was caused by the harness rather than the product, and is recorded
because it reads like a product failure from the outside: `npm run test:e2e` exited 1 with an
empty log. A second runner had been started while the first was still alive. Each picks a free
port, and neither knows about `.chaos/e2e-git-workspace/` or the endpoint's prompt log, which
both configs share, so each read a staged area and a prompt log belonging to the other run.
The suite is sequential inside one invocation (`e2e-runner.mjs` runs one config after
another); two invocations are not sequential with respect to each other, and
`apps/chaos-ui/README.md` now says so along with the `npm run build` that three of the specs
quietly require.

== 12. What this does not cover

The Provider on both sides of every test here is self-built and answers with fixed text, so
nothing in this batch measures suggestion *quality*; the guarantee is about the plumbing --
what is read, what is sent, what is stripped, and what is never executed. A truncated diff is
announced to the model and to the user, but the wording is still derived from the first 24 KiB
only. The suggestion is one request per click with no streaming into the box, so a slow
endpoint shows 建议生成中 until the answer lands; the hold-file branch proves the waiting
state and the late-arrival branch, not any particular timeout (there is none beyond the
endpoint's own). Provider configuration is still host-environment only -- the GUI
configuration form and keychain storage remain open rows. And the engine still answers
`CreateSession` with `SessionCreated` alone when it creates the fallback workspace, which is
now handled by the client re-asking rather than by the host pushing; a future client that does
neither will show the same empty sidebar. The reaping test in §8 is Linux-only by
construction -- it reads `/proc` and runs a `sh` script -- so on macOS and Windows the detach,
enroll and `kill_all()` calls still run but nothing there reports a verdict on them; those two
CI jobs compile the crate and run the other thirteen tests in the group.

Nothing here tests a refusal that arrives while two requests are in flight and expects the
second panel to hear about the second one, because the wire cannot support that promise:
`ServerMessage::Error` has a code and a message, no request id, and Safe Web Mode answers from
the socket before the engine sees a message at all. Attribution is therefore by in-flight flag
plus code, and when two refused requests are outstanding at the same instant, the order of the
reducer's branches decides which panel claims the first frame. The reducer test 'answers each
refused request with its own refusal, one panel at a time' pins what is actually promised --
every waiting panel eventually gets a frame, and none of them waits forever. Real per-request
correlation would be a protocol change (a field on `Error`, plus request bookkeeping in
`apps/chaos-ui/src/main.tsx`) and is not attempted in this batch.
